 321:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****   /* We don't bother to form the individual "smoothed" input pixel values;
 322:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * we can directly compute the output which is the average of the four
 323:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * smoothed values.  Each of the four member pixels contributes a fraction
 324:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * (1-8*SF) to its own smoothed image and a fraction SF to each of the three
 325:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * other smoothed pixels, therefore a total fraction (1-5*SF)/4 to the final
 326:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * output.  The four corner-adjacent neighbor pixels contribute a fraction
 327:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * SF to just one smoothed pixel, or SF/4 to the final output; while the
 328:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * eight edge-adjacent neighbors contribute SF to each of two smoothed
 329:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * pixels, or SF/2 overall.  In order to use integer arithmetic, these
ARM GAS  /tmp/ccq7GJi9.s 			page 22


 330:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * factors are scaled by 2^16 = 65536.
 331:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    * Also recall that SF = smoothing_factor / 1024.
 332:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcsample.c ****    */
